---
// Unlisted individual-ideation section for the ASU MEE 488 senior capstone (Team 9).
// Reached only by direct link shared with the team. Not in the nav, not in any
// sitemap, robots noindex. Employer stays off this page, same rule as the brief.
import Layout from "../../layouts/BareLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../components/Eyebrow.astro";

const IMG = "/ideation/img-r7v2";

const rubric = [
  ["Heading", "A short descriptive heading for the idea, with the member name."],
  ["Description", "A brief description of the idea. 10 points."],
  ["Sketch", "A very simple sketch or diagram. Hand drawn is acceptable. 10 points."],
  ["Rationale", "A short reason why the idea is a reasonable approach. 10 points."],
];

const names = [
  ["Last Hundred Feet", "Names the actual gap. Everything upstream of the last hundred feet is already solved."],
  ["Surefoot", "The capsule lands on its feet no matter what is underneath it."],
  ["TERRA", "Terrain Adaptive Rugged Airdrop."],
];
---
<Layout
  title="Individual ideation · ASU MEE 488 capstone, Team 9"
  description="Two candidate concepts for putting a package on the ground accurately when the aircraft cannot land. ASU MEE 488 senior capstone, Team 9."
  theme="asu"
>
  <div class="band" role="banner">
    <div class="shell band__row">
      <span class="band__school">Arizona State University</span>
      <span class="band__prog">Ira A. Fulton Schools of Engineering · MEE 488 Senior Design · Team 9</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="page-head shell">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="hero__text">
        <Eyebrow number="00" label="Individual ideation · unlisted" />
        <h1 class="page-head__h">Two ways to put a package on the ground where nothing can land.</h1>
        <p class="lead page-head__lead">
          Both concepts below were generated independently, before any group discussion, as the
          assignment asks. They are deliberately not variations on each other. The first removes the
          control system and moves the problem into structures. The second moves it again, into
          verification, and treats the condition the package arrives in rather than the place it
          arrives at as the thing worth solving.
        </p>
        <p class="meta"><span class="cred">Dontea Haliburton · MEE 488 · Team 9 · 9 September 2026</span></p>
      </div>
      <aside class="help" aria-labelledby="help-h">
        <p class="help__eyebrow">What this section has to contain</p>
        <h2 id="help-h" class="help__h">Individual ideation, 30 points, graded individually.</h2>
        <ul class="bare rubric">
          {rubric.map(([k, v]) => (
            <li><span class="rubric__k">{k}</span><span class="rubric__v">{v}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <Eyebrow number="01" label="Concept one" />
    <h2 class="section-h">Passive Terrain Adaptive Touchdown Capsule</h2>
    <p class="who">Dontea Haliburton</p>

    <p class="lead intro">
      A delivery capsule that carries no guidance and no propulsion, and is built so that it does not
      matter what it lands on or how it is turned when it gets there.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        It is released from the aircraft and steadied by a small drogue. Three passive subsystems then
        do all of the work. An outer cage of sprung legs opens during descent and conforms to whatever
        it touches, so slope, rock, snow and brush are all acceptable ground. A multi stage crushable
        core inside the cage absorbs the landing energy over a controlled stroke, sized for the worst
        case descent rate rather than the average one. The payload rides in a pod on a gimbal at the
        center, and the outer shape is ballasted so the capsule rights itself once it stops moving,
        which leaves the hatch and the locator beacon facing up every time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trade is deliberate. Instead of spending the budget on steering the package to a small
        target, the capsule spends it on surviving a much larger one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea1-hero.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea1-hero.jpg`} width="1400" height="1050" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"
          alt="Pencil sketch of a spherical capsule standing on four slender legs with flat oval foot pads, seen from three quarters above." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>The deployed capsule. Four sprung legs, spring loaded pivots, a conforming pad at each tip.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea1-sequence.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea1-sequence.jpg`} width="1400" height="788" loading="lazy"
          alt="Three pencil sketches in a row: the capsule under a small drogue with legs folded, the legs springing open during descent, and the capsule resting level on a steep rocky slope." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Release, descent, touchdown. On the slope each leg has stroked a different amount, so the body sits level on ground that is not.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig fig--narrow">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea1-section.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea1-section.jpg`} width="1400" height="1400" loading="lazy"
          alt="Pencil cross section of the capsule showing a thin outer shell, a thick hatched crushable ring, an inner payload sphere and a heavy ballast mass filling the bottom." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Section. Outer shell, crushable core drawn as hatching, payload pod, and the ballast that rights the capsule at rest.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="why">
      <h3 class="why__h">Why this is a reasonable approach</h3>
      <p>
        Every guided approach buys accuracy with cost, weight, power, and a dependence on GPS that does
        not hold in a canyon or under the smoke column of a fire. A guided system also fails in one
        place. If the guidance fails, the drop fails. A passive capsule has no such single point, and
        nothing on it needs to be charged or updated before a crew can use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also changes the requirement in a way that matches how users actually talk. It converts
        <em>land within a few meters of a chosen spot</em> into <em>land anywhere inside a safe area and
        still be usable</em>, which is closer to what a crew needs when the alternative is nothing at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is finally the version this team can genuinely build and test. Dropping a capsule from a known
        height onto measured ground is a student scale experiment we can run many times. Flight testing a
        guided vehicle is not. Our advisor's own framing of the problem, a terrain adaptive touchdown
        capsule, points directly at this architecture, and it puts the interesting engineering in
        structures and energy absorption rather than in software.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <Eyebrow number="02" label="Concept two" />
    <h2 class="section-h">Self Verifying Payload Capsule</h2>
    <p class="who">Dontea Haliburton</p>

    <p class="lead intro">
      A capsule whose job is not only to arrive, but to prove what condition it arrived in.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The payload is instrumented. A small impact logger and a temperature logger ride with the
        contents, and the capsule carries an indicator on the outside that can be read before anyone
        opens it. Green means every limit held for the whole flight and the landing. Amber means one
        limit was exceeded and the contents need checking. Red means the drop is not to be used. The
        same record is transmitted with the locator beacon, so the sender learns what the crew learns
        without the box being opened.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of the capsule can be simple. The innovation is that the package tells the truth about
        itself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea2-cutaway.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea2-cutaway.jpg`} width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy"
          alt="Pencil sketch of the capsule with a quarter of the shell cut away, showing a rectangular payload box suspended on shock isolators and two flat electronics modules on the floor beneath it." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>The payload box is suspended on shock isolators. The two flat modules beneath it are the impact and temperature loggers, riding with the contents rather than bolted to the shell.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea2-indicator.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea2-indicator.jpg`} width="1400" height="788" loading="lazy"
          alt="Three pencil sketches of the same round indicator lens set into the curved shell, shaded progressively darker from left to right." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>One lens, three readings. Clear means every limit held. Part shaded means one limit was exceeded and the contents need checking. Full dark means the drop is not to be used.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea2-hand.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea2-hand.jpg`} width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy"
          alt="Pencil sketch of a gloved hand pointing at the indicator lens on a closed capsule resting on broken rocky ground." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>The moment that matters. The capsule is still closed and the crew already knows whether what is inside can be used.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig fig--narrow">
      <picture>
        <source srcset={`${IMG}/idea2-exploded.webp`} type="image/webp" />
        <img src={`${IMG}/idea2-exploded.jpg`} width="1400" height="1867" loading="lazy"
          alt="Pencil exploded assembly sketch showing, from top to bottom, the upper shell hemisphere with the indicator lens, the payload box on isolators, two electronics modules, and the lower shell hemisphere with legs." />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Exploded along the assembly axis. Nothing about the shell has to change for the verification hardware to work.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="why">
      <h3 class="why__h">Why this is a reasonable approach</h3>
      <p>
        Blood products, medication and calibrated equipment are all ruined by conditions you cannot see
        from the outside. A crew that has to guess whether an air dropped kit is still good will either
        use something they should not have, or throw away something they should have used. Both outcomes
        are worse than a slower delivery.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our own interview set already asks what would make someone stop trusting a system, and that
        question exists because trust is the adoption barrier, not accuracy. A package that verifies
        itself removes the guessing at exactly the moment it matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        The idea comes out of nondestructive testing, which is my day job. That entire discipline exists
        to prove a part is still fit for service without taking it apart, and the same logic applies
        cleanly to a payload that has just absorbed an impact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="grid-2">
      <header>
        <Eyebrow number="03" label="How these differ" />
        <h2 class="section-h">Different architecture, not a different finish.</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The assignment scores whether ideas differ in approach or system architecture rather than in
          small variations. Concept one is deliberately the opposite of a guidance led design. It deletes
          the control system and puts the whole problem into structures and energy absorption. Concept
          two changes what is being solved altogether: the target is no longer where the package lands
          but whether anyone can trust it once it is down.
        </p>
        <p>
          They share a shell on purpose. If the team carries both forward they combine, and if the team
          carries one they are still separate answers rather than one answer twice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell">
    <Eyebrow number="04" label="Project name" />
    <h2 class="section-h">Three options for the team to pick from.</h2>
    <ol class="flow">
      {names.map(([n, d]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="flow__h">{n}</span>
          <span class="flow__d">{d}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="contact">
      Questions on either concept: <a href="mailto:dhalibur@asu.edu">dhalibur@asu.edu</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  /* ASU theme: maroon + gold on white, Arial/Helvetica per ASU brand. No logos. */
  html[data-theme="asu"] {
    --bg: #ffffff;
    --bg-elev: #f4f4f4;
    --ink: #191919;
    --ink-muted: #5c5c5c;
    --ink-faint: #8c8c8c;
    --rule: rgba(25, 25, 25, 0.14);
    --rule-strong: rgba(25, 25, 25, 0.38);
    --accent: #8c1d40;
    --accent-ink: #5c1129;
    --gold: #ffc627;
    --font-sans: Arial, "Helvetica Neue", Helvetica, sans-serif;
    --font-mono: Arial, "Helvetica Neue", Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }
  html[data-theme="asu"] .eyebrow { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.14em; }
  html[data-theme="asu"] .cred { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.02em; }
</style>

<style>
  .band { background: var(--accent); color: #fff; border-bottom: 6px solid var(--gold); }
  .band__row {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm); letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .band__school { font-weight: 700; }
  .band__prog { opacity: 0.92; }

  .page-head { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 5vw, 6rem) var(--space-6); }
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(18rem, 0.75fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .page-head__h { margin-top: var(--space-4); max-width: 22ch; position: relative; padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .page-head__h::after {
    content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; bottom: 0;
    width: 6rem; height: 0.5rem; background: var(--gold);
  }
  .page-head__lead { margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .meta { margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--ink-muted); }

  .help {
    border: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    position: sticky; top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .help__eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
  }
  .help__h { margin-top: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-lg); line-height: 1.25; }
  .rubric { margin-top: var(--space-4); display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .rubric li { display: grid; gap: 0.15rem; }
  .rubric__k {
    font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
  }
  .rubric__v { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--ink-muted); }

  .section-h { margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .who {
    margin-top: var(--space-2); color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm); letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
  .intro { margin-top: var(--space-5); max-width: 46ch; font-weight: 500; }
  .prose { margin-top: var(--space-4); max-width: 66ch; display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); }

  .fig {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    padding: var(--space-3);
  }
  .fig picture, .fig img { display: block; }
  .fig img { width: 100%; height: auto; background: #f2efe8; }
  .fig--narrow { max-width: 34rem; }
  .fig figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3); padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 4px solid var(--gold);
    font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--ink-muted); max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .why {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    border-left: 6px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 66ch;
    display: grid; gap: var(--space-4);
  }
  .why__h {
    font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
  }
  .why em { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }

  .grid-2 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-6); }
  @media (max-width: 860px) { .grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .flow { margin-top: var(--space-5); display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .flow li {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-top: var(--space-3); border-top: 4px solid var(--gold);
  }
  .flow__h { font-size: var(--text-lg); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .flow__d { color: var(--ink-muted); max-width: 34ch; }

  .contact { margin-top: var(--space-6); }
  .contact a { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; }
</style>
